21. Logging Levels

Standardize logging.

DEBUG

Detailed development diagnostics.

Do not depend on DEBUG for production triage.

INFO

Major lifecycle events:

File received
File registered
Transaction completed
Message published
Settlement completed

WARN

Recoverable/unexpected events:

Retry
Temporary dependency failure
Delayed response
Waiting for reconciliation

ERROR

Failed business/technical operation:

File validation failed
Message publish exhausted retries
Database operation failed
Settlement conversion failed

CRITICAL

System/business integrity or availability issue:

Data corruption
Unrecoverable processing failure
Critical dependency outage
Multi-DC consistency failure

⸻

22. Do Not Log Excessively

Do not log:

* entire request payloads
* entire response payloads
* complete files
* passwords
* tokens
* API keys
* connection strings
* certificates/private keys
* sensitive personal information
* full payment data unless explicitly required
* large message bodies.

Do not log the same exception at every application layer.

Preferred:

Lower layer:
add context and rethrow
Boundary:
log once with complete triage context

⸻

23. Exception Wrapping

When adding context, preserve the original exception.

Use:

throw new MessagePublishException(
    "Unable to publish reservation request.",
    ex);

Do not:

throw new Exception(ex.Message);

because this destroys valuable stack information.

⸻

24. Preserve Stack Trace

Never use patterns that destroy the original stack trace.

Use:

throw;

instead of:

throw ex;

when rethrowing the same exception.

⸻

25. Structured Logging

Do not build logs using string concatenation.

Avoid:

_logger.LogError(
    $"MQ failed for transaction {transactionId}");

Prefer structured properties:

_logger.LogError(
    exception,
    "MQ publish failed. ErrorCode={ErrorCode} TransactionId={TransactionId}",
    errorCode,
    transactionId);

Prefer centralized structured logging extensions so all required fields are consistently emitted.

⸻

26. Sensitive Data Protection

Before logging, classify data.

Never expose:

password
access token
refresh token
API key
private key
connection string
authorization header
cookie
secret

Mask sensitive business data where required.

Example:

accountNumber: ****1234

Do not place sensitive information inside:

exception.Message
rootCause
action
stackTrace

unless explicitly approved.

